# Who gets asked to review what.
#
# GitHub requests a review from the owner of any path a pull request touches.
# With a single maintainer that is simply "everything" — the file earns its place
# by making the request automatic rather than something to remember, and by being
# the thing to edit rather than invent when a second person joins.
#
# Syntax note: the *last* matching pattern wins, not the most specific one. So
# anything added below a broader rule overrides it, and order matters.

* @vikas-kumawatt

# ── Paths worth a second look ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Listed rather than assigned, because with one owner an extra rule would change
# nothing. They are here because reviewing them casually is how a subtle problem
# ships, and because when there is a second reviewer these are the lines that
# should name them:
#
#   server/controllers/authController.js  tokens, sessions, OTP, password reset
#   server/config/jwt.js                  which token may authenticate a request
#   server/config/origins.js              CORS and the CSRF guard, one list
#   server/utils/keyVault.js              BYOK encryption at rest
#   server/utils/signingSecret.js         the HMAC key and its domain separation
#   server/bots/actionValidator.js        re-validates everything a model returns
#   server/bots/outputModeration.js       every string a bot generates
#   server/bots/selfHosted.js             SSRF defence for owner endpoints
#   server/middleware/securityHeaders.js  headers on every response
#   frontend/public/_headers              the browser-facing CSP
#   .github/workflows/ci.yml              a merge here deploys to production
